 ## Background
-Monte Carlo methods for volume rendering originated from the need to accurately simulate light transport in participating media, avoiding the artifacts and limitations of uniform sampling [S1, unofficial]. The null-collision technique, also known as the virtual collision method, was introduced to handle heterogeneous media efficiently by sampling distances from a majorant distribution and rejecting non-scattering events [S2, unofficial]. The specific implementation described in the sources is a GPU-based approach using compute shaders for photon tracing and fragment shaders for ray casting, as seen in the `volumetric-ray-casting` and `vpt` repositories [S2, S3, unofficial].
+The Monte-Carlo approach was introduced as an alternative to uniform sampling, which may result in artifacts since a homogeneous material is assumed [S1, unofficial]. The null-collision technique, also known as the virtual collision method, was introduced to handle heterogeneous media efficiently by sampling distances from a majorant distribution and rejecting non-scattering events [S2, unofficial]. The specific implementation described in the sources is a GPU-based approach using fragment shaders for ray casting and photon tracing, as seen in the `volumetric-ray-casting` and `vpt` repositories [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
